\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces (a) A sketch of the effect a gravitational wave has on a Michelson interferomter. The positions of the test masses, ETMX and ETMY, at different points during the gravitational wave's period is shown by the blue and pink lines. The difference in phase shift between the fields in the two arms affects the intensity of the light at the AS port. By detecting this light with a photodiode, the gravitational wave strain $h$ can be determined. (b) To reach the sensitivity needed to detect gravitational wave, several mirrors need to be added to the basic Michelson interferometer: ITMX and ITMY boost the signal generarted in the arms, the PRM increases the power at the beam splitter, and the SRM can be used to shape the quantum noise of the interferometer.\relax }}{20}{figure.caption.18}}
\newlabel{fig:detectorchaptersimplelayout}{{2.1}{20}{(a) A sketch of the effect a gravitational wave has on a Michelson interferomter. The positions of the test masses, ETMX and ETMY, at different points during the gravitational wave's period is shown by the blue and pink lines. The difference in phase shift between the fields in the two arms affects the intensity of the light at the AS port. By detecting this light with a photodiode, the gravitational wave strain $h$ can be determined. (b) To reach the sensitivity needed to detect gravitational wave, several mirrors need to be added to the basic Michelson interferometer: ITMX and ITMY boost the signal generarted in the arms, the PRM increases the power at the beam splitter, and the SRM can be used to shape the quantum noise of the interferometer.\relax }{figure.caption.18}{}}
