\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces A reproduction of the intensity noise requirement from \cite  {PSL_requirements}. The outer loop requirement is shown in blue and the inner loop is shown by the dashed orange line. To lock the IMC, the inner loop requirement must be met. At frequencies below 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the outer loop requirement is determined by the radiation pressure noise that is expected to arise due to imbalances in the arm power. The requirement is most stringent at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. The requirement relaxes above 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz due to the PRC cavity pole. The inner loop requirement is less extreme than the outer loop as it is only used to stabilise the laser in the initial locking sequence.\relax }}{62}{figure.caption.81}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterissrequirement}{{3.11}{62}{A reproduction of the intensity noise requirement from \cite {PSL_requirements}. The outer loop requirement is shown in blue and the inner loop is shown by the dashed orange line. To lock the IMC, the inner loop requirement must be met. At frequencies below 10\,Hz, the outer loop requirement is determined by the radiation pressure noise that is expected to arise due to imbalances in the arm power. The requirement is most stringent at 10\,Hz. The requirement relaxes above 10\,Hz due to the PRC cavity pole. The inner loop requirement is less extreme than the outer loop as it is only used to stabilise the laser in the initial locking sequence.\relax }{figure.caption.81}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces The intensity stabilisation concept involves two loops. The inner loop is responsible for stabilising the laser so that the IMC can lock. The outer loop is used to stabilise the laser for observation mode. The two loops sense the laser intensity noise at different points. The inner loop uses a low power beam, $\sim 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {mW}$, transmitted by the PMC. The outer loop uses a higher power, $\sim 350\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {mW}$, pick-off from the main beam. An AOM will be used to actuate on the laser's power.\relax }}{63}{figure.caption.82}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterissconcept}{{3.12}{63}{The intensity stabilisation concept involves two loops. The inner loop is responsible for stabilising the laser so that the IMC can lock. The outer loop is used to stabilise the laser for observation mode. The two loops sense the laser intensity noise at different points. The inner loop uses a low power beam, $\sim 1\,\rm {mW}$, transmitted by the PMC. The outer loop uses a higher power, $\sim 350\,\rm {mW}$, pick-off from the main beam. An AOM will be used to actuate on the laser's power.\relax }{figure.caption.82}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{63}{section*.85}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces The free running noise of the NPRO and each amplifier is shown. The noise after the PMC was also measured. There is a forest of 4\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz lines due to the Beckhoff control computer in the amplifier and PMC noise curves. Due to the non linear nature of the saturated amplifiers, at high frequencies the output beam intensity noise decreases relative to the input beam. The PMC couples length noise to intensity noise between 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz and 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz. This excess can be tuned away in the PDH servo.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.86}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrn}{{3.13}{64}{The free running noise of the NPRO and each amplifier is shown. The noise after the PMC was also measured. There is a forest of 4\,Hz lines due to the Beckhoff control computer in the amplifier and PMC noise curves. Due to the non linear nature of the saturated amplifiers, at high frequencies the output beam intensity noise decreases relative to the input beam. The PMC couples length noise to intensity noise between 100\,Hz and 10\,kHz. This excess can be tuned away in the PDH servo.\relax }{figure.caption.86}{}}
