\newlabel{sec:ligolaserchapterservomodel}{{3.5.4.1}{73}{Model of the Open-Loop Transfer Function}{subsubsection.3.5.4.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces The open loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in Figure \ref  {fig:ligolaserchapteroltfmeasurement}. The unity gain point is at 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\kilo \hertz } with 70\si {\degree } of phase margin. The shaping of the servo is $1/f$ at the unity gain point, but to achieve higher gain at low frequency, the servo has a $/f^3$ slope below the unity gain point. The measurement saturated below 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. The model and measurement do not agree well below a few kHz due to the measurement saturating.\relax }}{74}{figure.caption.97}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteropenlooptransferfunction}{{3.19}{74}{The open loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in Figure \ref {fig:ligolaserchapteroltfmeasurement}. The unity gain point is at 48\,\si {\kilo \hertz } with 70\si {\degree } of phase margin. The shaping of the servo is $1/f$ at the unity gain point, but to achieve higher gain at low frequency, the servo has a $/f^3$ slope below the unity gain point. The measurement saturated below 100\,Hz. The model and measurement do not agree well below a few kHz due to the measurement saturating.\relax }{figure.caption.97}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.5.5}Measurement of the Intensity Stabilisation of a 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\watt } Beam to the Inner Loop Requirement}{75}{subsection.3.5.5}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Model of the intensity stabilisation servo expressed in terms of the complex angular frequency $s = \omega + i\sigma $. Note that in reality the gain is distributed amongst each block; in this model all the components have unity gain at DC. The additional integrators have been expressed as a separate block to the transitional integrators with a pole that cancels a zero in the transitional integrators; the zero in this additional integrator transfer function was chosen to be below any frequency of interest and does not exist in the servo. It was included here to make modelling the servo more clear. \relax }}{76}{table.caption.98}}
\newlabel{tab:ligolaserchapterservomodel}{{3.1}{76}{Model of the intensity stabilisation servo expressed in terms of the complex angular frequency $s = \omega + i\sigma $. Note that in reality the gain is distributed amongst each block; in this model all the components have unity gain at DC. The additional integrators have been expressed as a separate block to the transitional integrators with a pole that cancels a zero in the transitional integrators; the zero in this additional integrator transfer function was chosen to be below any frequency of interest and does not exist in the servo. It was included here to make modelling the servo more clear. \relax }{table.caption.98}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.20}{\ignorespaces The free-running noise of the laser is shown in red. The noise of the light transmitted by the PMC is shown in blue; this meets the requirement that is shown by the dashed black line. The noise level is determined by the in loop noise, shown in green, the shot noise and other technical noise sources. The sum of these is shown by the dotted brown line. Below 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the noise is shot noise limited. There is a discrepancy between the noise budget and the measured noise. This is likely due to dust falling through the beam or scattered light. Near the unity gain point of the servo, the noise is enhanced by the servo due to gain peaking.\relax }}{78}{figure.caption.99}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomstabilisationnoise}{{3.20}{78}{The free-running noise of the laser is shown in red. The noise of the light transmitted by the PMC is shown in blue; this meets the requirement that is shown by the dashed black line. The noise level is determined by the in loop noise, shown in green, the shot noise and other technical noise sources. The sum of these is shown by the dotted brown line. Below 100\,Hz, the noise is shot noise limited. There is a discrepancy between the noise budget and the measured noise. This is likely due to dust falling through the beam or scattered light. Near the unity gain point of the servo, the noise is enhanced by the servo due to gain peaking.\relax }{figure.caption.99}{}}
