\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Sketches of the WS representation for a Gaussian beam interacting with different parts of a simple optical layout consisting of two active lenses with powers $S_{1,2} \pm \Delta S_{1,2}$. The left hand axis shows the WS plane at Lens 1. The input beam $q_1$ is indicated by a red dot, and the effect of the lens on $q_1$ is shown by the dark green arrow $S_1$. This results in a new mode $q_2$ which is shown as a blue dot. A light green arrow indicates the actuation of Lens 1. The right hand axis shows the WS plane at Lens 2. The input mode at Lens 2, $q_3$, is shown as a purple dot, and the output mode, $q_4$, is shown as a pink dot. The static and dynamic power of Lens 2 is indicated by the orange and yellow lines. As Lens 1 and Lens 2 are separated by $45\si {\degree }$ of Gouy Phase, the actuation provided by Lens 1 results in a change in the beam parameter which is orthogonal to the actuation provided by Lens 2, provided that the actuations are small enough. Beneath the two WS space diagrams is a sketch of the optical axis and where the modes are along it.\relax }}{178}{figure.caption.238}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterwssketch}{{6.5}{178}{Sketches of the WS representation for a Gaussian beam interacting with different parts of a simple optical layout consisting of two active lenses with powers $S_{1,2} \pm \Delta S_{1,2}$. The left hand axis shows the WS plane at Lens 1. The input beam $q_1$ is indicated by a red dot, and the effect of the lens on $q_1$ is shown by the dark green arrow $S_1$. This results in a new mode $q_2$ which is shown as a blue dot. A light green arrow indicates the actuation of Lens 1. The right hand axis shows the WS plane at Lens 2. The input mode at Lens 2, $q_3$, is shown as a purple dot, and the output mode, $q_4$, is shown as a pink dot. The static and dynamic power of Lens 2 is indicated by the orange and yellow lines. As Lens 1 and Lens 2 are separated by $45\si {\degree }$ of Gouy Phase, the actuation provided by Lens 1 results in a change in the beam parameter which is orthogonal to the actuation provided by Lens 2, provided that the actuations are small enough. Beneath the two WS space diagrams is a sketch of the optical axis and where the modes are along it.\relax }{figure.caption.238}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces This is the WS space at the OMx2 plane. The effect of OMx2 in the plane of OMx2 is to move a mode vertically in WS space. After reflection from OMx2, a mode must be at least $98\%$ mode-matched to the OMC as there are no further active mode-matching elements in the layout of HAM6. Modes with at least $98\%$ are shown by the red region. The blue region represents which modes can be reached from the red region by either $S_{\rm  {OMx2}}\pm \Delta S$, and was constructed by applying transformations to the modes in the red region. The red region was split into two parts, `upper' and `lower', indicated by the dashed line that connects the two extremes in width of the region. To create the lower edge of the blue region, modes in the lower edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm  {OMx2}}-\Delta S$ applied to them. To construct the upper edge of the blue region, the upper edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm  {OMx2}}+\Delta S$ applied to them.\relax }}{179}{figure.caption.239}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5fomx1omx2planes}{{6.6}{179}{This is the WS space at the OMx2 plane. The effect of OMx2 in the plane of OMx2 is to move a mode vertically in WS space. After reflection from OMx2, a mode must be at least $98\%$ mode-matched to the OMC as there are no further active mode-matching elements in the layout of HAM6. Modes with at least $98\%$ are shown by the red region. The blue region represents which modes can be reached from the red region by either $S_{\rm {OMx2}}\pm \Delta S$, and was constructed by applying transformations to the modes in the red region. The red region was split into two parts, `upper' and `lower', indicated by the dashed line that connects the two extremes in width of the region. To create the lower edge of the blue region, modes in the lower edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm {OMx2}}-\Delta S$ applied to them. To construct the upper edge of the blue region, the upper edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm {OMx2}}+\Delta S$ applied to them.\relax }{figure.caption.239}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Left panel: the set of modes that could be present at the SRM, as described in Chapter \ref  {chapter:simulation_finesse}, is shown by the purple-blue-green-yellow curve. The pink region shows which modes can reach 98\% mode-matching by using the AWC in HAM6. The contours indicate the mode-matching between a mode $(W,S)$ and the OMC mode. Right panel: this shows the same modes as the left panel, except they have been transformed by the optical layout to the plane of the OMC waist. Modes within the pink region can be actuated on so they move to the goal region, which is indicated with a hatched area.\relax }}{180}{figure.caption.240}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5fmodessrmomcmode matchingsuccess}{{6.7}{180}{Left panel: the set of modes that could be present at the SRM, as described in Chapter \ref {chapter:simulation_finesse}, is shown by the purple-blue-green-yellow curve. The pink region shows which modes can reach 98\% mode-matching by using the AWC in HAM6. The contours indicate the mode-matching between a mode $(W,S)$ and the OMC mode. Right panel: this shows the same modes as the left panel, except they have been transformed by the optical layout to the plane of the OMC waist. Modes within the pink region can be actuated on so they move to the goal region, which is indicated with a hatched area.\relax }{figure.caption.240}{}}
